          objective: 'Win chips/money by winning pots in each of the five poker variants.',
          gameplay: "HORSE = rotation of Hold'em, Omaha, Razz, Stud, Stud Eight-or-better. Each variant 1 orbit; switch after each. Same betting structure. Lowest chip count at end loses.",
          keyRules: [
            "All five games are played as fixed-limit. Hold'em and Omaha use blinds; Razz, Stud, and Stud 8 use antes and a compulsory bring-in.",
            'Rotation is fixed: H then O then R then S then E. A complete round of each game is played (every player has one deal or button) before switching.',
            "Hand rankings and showdown rules follow the current variant: standard high for Hold'em and Stud; Omaha 8 and Stud 8 split high and low (eight or better for low).",
            'With more than seven players, the last in order sit out for stud games so the deck does not run out.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Learn the fundamentals of each individual game before attempting HORSE. Focus on starting hand selection for each variant.',
          intermediate: "Adjust your strategy as the game rotates. For example, tight-aggressive play works in Hold'em, but Razz requires different hand values.",
          advanced: "Exploit opponents' weaknesses in specific games. If an opponent is weak in Razz, apply pressure when that game is in rotation.",
          tips: [
            "Razz and Stud Hi/Lo are often the weakest games for Hold'em specialists. Study these carefully.",
            "In Omaha Hi/Lo, always aim to 'scoop' (win both high and low) rather than just half the pot.",
            "Track which game is currently being playedâ€”it's easy to forget during long sessions.",
